1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,140
Imagine you could send a
message faster than light.

2
00:00:03,608 --> 00:00:06,248
You send it to a friend
on a distant spaceship.

3
00:00:06,908 --> 00:00:09,648
They read it and send
a reply, just as fast.

4
00:00:10,062 --> 00:00:13,122
And the reply reaches you
before you've sent the message.

5
00:00:14,249 --> 00:00:17,449
That's not a trick of the clocks,
and it's not science fiction.

6
00:00:17,969 --> 00:00:21,449
It's what the equations of
relativity say could happen.

7
00:00:22,427 --> 00:00:26,287
In our last video, we asked what it
would take to travel faster than light.

8
00:00:26,807 --> 00:00:29,087
Warp drives. Wormholes.

9
00:00:29,317 --> 00:00:31,147
Exotic matter.

10
00:00:31,851 --> 00:00:33,791
This time, the harder question.

11
00:00:34,491 --> 00:00:37,891
Why do physicists think the
universe might not allow it at all?

12
00:00:40,071 --> 00:00:42,011
First, the speed limit itself.

13
00:00:42,451 --> 00:00:44,391
Because it isn't a rule of thumb.

14
00:00:44,471 --> 00:00:46,071
It has been measured.

15
00:00:46,763 --> 00:00:51,063
In 1964, at MIT, William
Bertozzi fired electrons

16
00:00:51,293 --> 00:00:54,463
down a tube and timed them
over about eight metres.

17
00:00:55,263 --> 00:00:57,763
Then he gave them more energy. And more.

18
00:00:58,283 --> 00:01:00,403
In the end, thirty times as much.

19
00:01:01,143 --> 00:01:03,903
At the lowest energy,
the electrons moved at

20
00:01:04,133 --> 00:01:06,723
about eighty-seven per
cent of the speed of light.

21
00:01:07,183 --> 00:01:09,143
With thirty times the energy?

22
00:01:09,803 --> 00:01:13,383
So close to the speed of light that his
timing couldn't tell the difference.

23
00:01:14,193 --> 00:01:16,573
Newton's physics had a different answer.

24
00:01:17,333 --> 00:01:19,293
By his rules, the fastest
of them should have

25
00:01:19,523 --> 00:01:22,013
been moving several
times faster than light.

26
00:01:22,496 --> 00:01:24,496
And the energy hadn't gone missing.

27
00:01:25,116 --> 00:01:28,776
He caught the electrons in an
aluminium disc and measured the heat.

28
00:01:29,136 --> 00:01:30,516
It was all there.

29
00:01:30,951 --> 00:01:32,871
The extra energy went in.

30
00:01:33,451 --> 00:01:38,271
The electrons crept closer to light
speed, and stopped short. That's the wall.

31
00:01:38,944 --> 00:01:42,424
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

32
00:01:43,044 --> 00:01:44,664
It really helps the channel.

33
00:01:46,834 --> 00:01:48,674
But a wall isn't a reason.

34
00:01:49,214 --> 00:01:51,074
Why should there be a limit at all?

35
00:01:51,732 --> 00:01:55,672
The answer hides in something we
never think about: the word "now".

36
00:01:56,372 --> 00:01:59,312
Picture a long train
racing past a station.

37
00:01:59,992 --> 00:02:02,232
Lightning strikes both ends of it.

38
00:02:02,785 --> 00:02:05,925
A woman on the platform stands
halfway between the strikes.

39
00:02:06,765 --> 00:02:08,825
Both flashes reach her together.

40
00:02:09,365 --> 00:02:11,225
For her, the strikes were simultaneous.

41
00:02:12,429 --> 00:02:14,889
A passenger sits in the
middle of the train.

42
00:02:15,629 --> 00:02:19,349
But she's moving towards the front
flash and away from the back one.

43
00:02:19,529 --> 00:02:22,029
So the front flash reaches her first.

44
00:02:22,319 --> 00:02:25,479
And because light travels at
the same speed for everyone, the

45
00:02:25,709 --> 00:02:29,219
passenger concludes, correctly,
that the front was struck first.

46
00:02:29,881 --> 00:02:31,601
Neither of them is wrong.

47
00:02:32,241 --> 00:02:34,341
This was Einstein's own example.

48
00:02:34,421 --> 00:02:39,241
Whether two distant events happen at the
same time depends on how you're moving.

49
00:02:40,416 --> 00:02:41,996
That sounds dangerous.

50
00:02:42,536 --> 00:02:44,936
Could someone see an
effect before its cause?

51
00:02:45,719 --> 00:02:47,559
No. And this is the key.

52
00:02:48,133 --> 00:02:51,193
The order can only flip for
events so far apart that

53
00:02:51,423 --> 00:02:54,333
not even light could get from
one to the other in time.

54
00:02:54,527 --> 00:02:58,487
If one event can affect the other,
everyone agrees which came first.

55
00:02:59,210 --> 00:03:03,550
In relativity, the light-speed limit
is what keeps cause before effect.

56
00:03:06,170 --> 00:03:07,730
Now let's break it.

57
00:03:08,580 --> 00:03:13,480
Alice is on Earth. Bob is on a
spaceship, one light-year away, flying

58
00:03:13,710 --> 00:03:16,520
further out at ninety per
cent of the speed of light.

59
00:03:17,169 --> 00:03:20,929
Alice sends him a message that
travels at twice the speed of light.

60
00:03:21,574 --> 00:03:24,034
For Alice, nothing strange happens.

61
00:03:24,534 --> 00:03:28,314
The message leaves, and almost
eleven months later, it reaches Bob.

62
00:03:28,647 --> 00:03:32,327
But the two events are so far apart
that light couldn't connect them.

63
00:03:32,967 --> 00:03:35,787
So Bob and Alice don't have
to agree on their order.

64
00:03:36,425 --> 00:03:40,265
And in Bob's frame, the message
arrives before Alice sent it.

65
00:03:41,387 --> 00:03:44,927
That's unsettling. But
it isn't a paradox yet.

66
00:03:45,781 --> 00:03:47,201
Now Bob replies.

67
00:03:48,021 --> 00:03:51,601
Same kind of signal, twice the speed
of light, measured from his ship.

68
00:03:52,437 --> 00:03:54,657
Work it through, and
his reply reaches Earth

69
00:03:54,887 --> 00:03:57,597
about five months before
Alice sent her message.

70
00:03:58,493 --> 00:04:02,053
Alice receives an answer to a
question she hasn't asked yet.

71
00:04:03,242 --> 00:04:06,542
Einstein himself noticed this in 1907.

72
00:04:07,062 --> 00:04:12,875
In 1970, three physicists gave it a
name: the tachyonic antitelephone.

73
00:04:13,375 --> 00:04:18,255
And here's the problem. Suppose the
reply says: "Don't send the message."

74
00:04:19,198 --> 00:04:22,178
If Alice obeys, there's
nothing for Bob to answer.

75
00:04:22,838 --> 00:04:24,878
So where did the reply come from?

76
00:04:25,397 --> 00:04:27,817
An effect has prevented its own cause.

77
00:04:29,707 --> 00:04:33,667
Combine faster-than-light signals
with the usual rules of relativity,

78
00:04:34,207 --> 00:04:37,627
and you can construct a message
that arrives before it was sent.

79
00:04:37,873 --> 00:04:40,673
That is a serious problem
for cause and effect.

80
00:04:41,513 --> 00:04:45,453
Not proof that every imaginable
shortcut through spacetime must fail.

81
00:04:45,699 --> 00:04:48,339
And it doesn't matter what
the signal is made of.

82
00:04:48,979 --> 00:04:50,859
Radio, particles, anything.

83
00:04:51,459 --> 00:04:55,839
All that counts is that it's faster than
light, and that the usual rules hold.

84
00:04:58,419 --> 00:05:01,559
Now, physics never proves
that something is impossible.

85
00:05:02,379 --> 00:05:04,439
A new theory could change the rules.

86
00:05:04,794 --> 00:05:06,474
But it would have to
explain why relativity

87
00:05:06,704 --> 00:05:09,434
has passed every test we've given it.

88
00:05:09,525 --> 00:05:11,445
And the tests are very good.

89
00:05:12,025 --> 00:05:13,985
Modern experiments have
searched for any difference

90
00:05:14,215 --> 00:05:16,605
in the speed of light
between different directions.

91
00:05:17,237 --> 00:05:20,937
Down to about one part in a
billion billion, they found none.

92
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:24,980
And once, it looked as though
something had broken through.

93
00:05:25,608 --> 00:05:31,048
In 2011, the OPERA experiment sent
neutrinos from CERN, near Geneva, to a

94
00:05:31,278 --> 00:05:35,848
lab under a mountain in Italy, seven
hundred and thirty kilometres away.

95
00:05:36,520 --> 00:05:41,520
They arrived about sixty billionths
of a second early. Faster than light.

96
00:05:42,450 --> 00:05:44,330
And it wasn't a fluke of statistics.

97
00:05:45,290 --> 00:05:47,570
The result was far too strong for that.

98
00:05:48,050 --> 00:05:50,910
Whatever was wrong was
wrong every single time.

99
00:05:51,467 --> 00:05:53,487
The team didn't claim a revolution.

100
00:05:54,267 --> 00:05:57,507
They published the result and
asked others to find the mistake.

101
00:05:58,243 --> 00:06:01,103
Within months, the investigation
found problems in the

102
00:06:01,333 --> 00:06:05,063
timing system, including a
faulty fibre-optic connection.

103
00:06:05,712 --> 00:06:07,932
Once the timing was
corrected and the measurement

104
00:06:08,162 --> 00:06:11,532
repeated, the faster-than-light
result disappeared.

105
00:06:12,293 --> 00:06:15,273
The lesson isn't that
relativity can never be wrong.

106
00:06:15,933 --> 00:06:19,113
It's that an extraordinary
result has to survive checking.

107
00:06:19,513 --> 00:06:21,173
This one didn't.

108
00:06:23,333 --> 00:06:25,993
So signals can't simply outrun light.

109
00:06:26,653 --> 00:06:29,273
But in our last video,
we met a different idea.

110
00:06:29,938 --> 00:06:34,018
A wormhole. A shortcut between
two places, where nothing has

111
00:06:34,248 --> 00:06:36,858
to outrun light, because
the path is shorter.

112
00:06:37,267 --> 00:06:40,607
And in 1988, Kip Thorne
and two colleagues showed

113
00:06:40,837 --> 00:06:43,487
how a wormhole could be
turned into a time machine.

114
00:06:44,125 --> 00:06:46,445
It takes one trick we've already seen.

115
00:06:46,944 --> 00:06:49,184
A clock that travels
close to light speed runs

116
00:06:49,414 --> 00:06:52,004
slow compared with one that stays at home.

117
00:06:52,544 --> 00:06:56,144
Start with a wormhole whose
two mouths sit side by side.

118
00:06:56,364 --> 00:06:58,664
Through the tunnel, their clocks agree.

119
00:06:59,319 --> 00:07:02,009
Now take one mouth on a
long trip, at ninety-nine

120
00:07:02,239 --> 00:07:05,359
per cent of the speed of
light, and bring it back.

121
00:07:06,029 --> 00:07:08,069
On Earth, ten years have passed.

122
00:07:08,489 --> 00:07:11,429
For the travelling mouth,
less than a year and a half.

123
00:07:12,100 --> 00:07:15,480
But through the tunnel, the clocks
at the two mouths still agree.

124
00:07:15,764 --> 00:07:18,264
So step into the mouth that
travelled, and you come

125
00:07:18,494 --> 00:07:20,984
out of the other one more
than eight years earlier.

126
00:07:22,904 --> 00:07:26,224
A time shift between the mouths
isn't yet a time machine.

127
00:07:26,504 --> 00:07:28,924
That happens when the shift
is longer than light needs

128
00:07:29,154 --> 00:07:32,524
to get from one mouth to
the other the ordinary way.

129
00:07:32,728 --> 00:07:35,148
Then you can step out
in the past, walk back

130
00:07:35,378 --> 00:07:38,108
to the first mouth, and
arrive before you left.

131
00:07:39,355 --> 00:07:43,155
None of this tells us how to build
such a wormhole, or keep it open.

132
00:07:43,755 --> 00:07:45,415
It's a solution on paper.

133
00:07:45,937 --> 00:07:49,257
But it shows that the paradox
isn't only about signals.

134
00:07:49,997 --> 00:07:53,157
Any shortcut through spacetime
may lead to the same place.

135
00:07:55,927 --> 00:07:57,847
So does the universe forbid it?

136
00:07:58,207 --> 00:08:00,027
There are two famous answers.

137
00:08:01,225 --> 00:08:04,905
In 1992, Stephen Hawking
proposed that the laws of

138
00:08:05,135 --> 00:08:08,285
physics themselves prevent
time machines from forming.

139
00:08:08,651 --> 00:08:11,771
His idea was that as a wormhole
came close to becoming a time

140
00:08:12,001 --> 00:08:15,451
machine, the restless energy
of empty space would build up

141
00:08:15,681 --> 00:08:19,831
inside it without limit, and stop
the time machine from forming.

142
00:08:20,195 --> 00:08:23,075
He called it the chronology
protection conjecture.

143
00:08:23,833 --> 00:08:26,633
And he pointed out, half
jokingly, that we have not

144
00:08:26,863 --> 00:08:29,653
been invaded by hordes of
tourists from the future.

145
00:08:30,443 --> 00:08:32,263
It was a joke, not a proof.

146
00:08:33,203 --> 00:08:35,943
In 2009, he threw a party
for time travellers,

147
00:08:36,173 --> 00:08:39,163
and sent out the
invitations only afterwards.

148
00:08:40,456 --> 00:08:41,676
Nobody came.

149
00:08:43,936 --> 00:08:47,296
The Russian physicist Igor
Novikov answered differently.

150
00:08:47,821 --> 00:08:51,101
Maybe the past can be visited,
but it can't be changed.

151
00:08:52,264 --> 00:08:56,424
The physicist Joseph Polchinski
challenged the idea with a billiard ball.

152
00:08:56,904 --> 00:09:00,524
Send it into a wormhole, so
that it comes out in the past

153
00:09:00,715 --> 00:09:02,975
and knocks its younger self off course.

154
00:09:03,375 --> 00:09:06,715
Then it never enters
the wormhole. A paradox.

155
00:09:07,757 --> 00:09:10,837
But when Thorne and two colleagues
worked through the equations,

156
00:09:11,057 --> 00:09:13,917
they found that the collision
could also happen differently.

157
00:09:14,501 --> 00:09:15,821
A glancing blow.

158
00:09:16,521 --> 00:09:20,261
The younger ball is nudged, not
stopped, and still enters the wormhole.

159
00:09:21,049 --> 00:09:22,929
For every starting point they tried, they

160
00:09:23,159 --> 00:09:25,549
found at least one
self-consistent history.

161
00:09:26,509 --> 00:09:28,349
For some, there was more than one.

162
00:09:30,559 --> 00:09:32,359
Neither answer is proven.

163
00:09:32,859 --> 00:09:34,479
Hawking's is a conjecture.

164
00:09:35,299 --> 00:09:37,679
Novikov's is a principle,
not an observation.

165
00:09:39,017 --> 00:09:42,617
So for now, the speed of light
isn't just a limit on our engines.

166
00:09:43,497 --> 00:09:46,197
It may be what keeps causes
before their effects.

167
00:09:47,565 --> 00:09:50,585
Either the universe never
lets a time machine form...

168
00:09:51,003 --> 00:09:54,463
or it allows only histories that
never contradict themselves.

169
00:09:55,765 --> 00:09:57,595
We don't yet know which, if either.
